2. Commercial use needs a commercial licence

   Running Sluurp to operate a business, to earn money from an application
   built on it, or to build or host applications for paying clients is
   commercial use. Commercial use is allowed only on servers covered by a
   paid commercial licence, one licence per server, on the terms of that
   licence. Development on your own computer does not need one.

3. Commercial licences are yours alone

   A commercial licence is granted to the person or business that bought
   it, for its own use. It may not be resold, rented, lent, given away,
   shared or otherwise transferred to anyone else, alone or with anything
   else, without the written agreement of the Sluurp authors. You may move a
   licence from one of your own servers to another, as long as it covers no
   more servers at once than you paid for.

   Licences for additional servers, sold at a lower price, extend a first
   full-price licence. They may be used only by the same person or business
   that holds that first licence, on its own servers. They may not be bought
   for, used by or passed on to anyone else: another business, a client, a
   partner, or a company in the same group that is a separate legal entity.

4. What you may not do

   You may not modify, decompile, disassemble or reverse engineer the
   binaries, except where the law allows it despite this term; remove or
